{"purpose":"Record that RSF package emission is not atomic despite documentation claiming it is, so an interrupted export leaves a corrupt artifact, and state the acceptance criteria.","content":"## 1. Claim versus code\nThe eka-cli README states the export path \"emits atomically ... never leaves a partial package behind\". The implementation does not support that claim for I/O failure.\n\n`eka-core/exchange/write.go` writes the package in place:\n\n```go\nfunc writeZIP(path string, entries []entry) error {\n    if err := checkAllEntryNames(entries); err != nil { return err }\n    ...\n    f, err := os.Create(path)        // truncates the destination immediately\n    ...\n}\n\nfunc writeDir(dir string, entries []entry) error {\n    ...\n    for _, e := range entries {\n        if err := os.WriteFile(path, e.data, 0o644); err != nil { ... }   // one file at a time\n    }\n}\n```\n\n## 2. What the claim actually holds for\nAll entry names are validated before any file is created (`checkAllEntryNames`), so a *refusal* leaves nothing behind. That is the case the documentation describes and it is genuinely correct. What is not covered: a crash, disk-full, or permission failure *during* writing. In those cases a truncated `.ekapkg` or a half-populated package directory remains at the destination path, and a consumer cannot distinguish it from a complete package except by validating contents.\n\n## 3. Why this matters now\nExchange packages are the transport between repositories and the workspace. A partially written package that is later imported produces confusing parse errors far from the failure site. The snapshot transport path already uses a staging-directory-plus-rename discipline for this reason; export does not.\n\n## 4. Acceptance criteria\n- Both emission shapes (ZIP and directory) write to a temporary sibling path and publish to the destination with a single atomic rename on success only.\n- Any failure during writing leaves the destination path in its prior state, exactly as the documented refusal path does.\n- A test simulates a mid-write failure (for example an unwritable target or an injected error) and asserts the destination is either absent or holds the previously complete package.\n- The documentation statement is either backed by the above or narrowed to the refusal case it actually describes."}